\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Extended Limitations}
\label{sec:extended_limitations}

\emph{Prompt-chain versus retrain-chain.}
Retrain-chain validation (Pythia-1.4B LoRA, 5 generations) yields $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ (Table~\ref{tab:retrain_ci}), far exceeding prompt-chain $\Kempirical{=}2$.
All pairwise CIs have lower bounds well above $\theta{=}0.60$ ($p < 0.001$), statistically confirming $\Kempirical{\geq}4$.
The elevated signal arises from cumulative model-adaptation artifacts (vocabulary narrowing, repetition bias) rather than text-intrinsic degradation; prompt-chain $\Kempirical$ is the operationally relevant boundary for data curation.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Retrain-chain pairwise $\auc$ with bootstrap 95\% CIs (Pythia-1.4B LoRA, 5 generations). All CI lower bounds $\gg \theta{=}0.60$.}
\label{tab:retrain_ci}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Pair} & $\auc$ & \textbf{95\% CI} & $p$ \\
\midrule
0v1 & 0.9842 & $[0.9823,\,0.9860]$ & ${<}0.001$ \\
1v2 & 0.9471 & $[0.9432,\,0.9509]$ & ${<}0.001$ \\
2v3 & 0.8888 & $[0.8832,\,0.8943]$ & ${<}0.001$ \\
3v4 & 0.7994 & $[0.7920,\,0.8067]$ & ${<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\emph{Threshold sensitivity.}
Table~\ref{tab:theta_sensitivity} reports $\Kempirical$ across $\theta \in [0.55, 0.70]$ for representative configurations including arXiv and cross-scorer conditions.
Qwen-continuation's 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.2\,pp; all ten boundary comparisons yield permutation $p < 0.001$.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{$\Kempirical$ as a function of $\theta$ across conditions. The \textbf{domain ordering} (arXiv $>$ web $\geq$ cross-scorer) and \textbf{bounded range} ($\Kempirical{\leq}4$) are preserved across $\theta \in [0.55, 0.70]$. The headline result (web $\Kempirical{=}2$, arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$) holds for $\theta \in [0.575, 0.625]$.}
\label{tab:theta_sensitivity}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Config} & $\theta{=}0.55$ & $\theta{=}0.60$ & $\theta{=}0.625$ & $\theta{=}0.70$ \\
\midrule
C4 (web) & Qwen-rw      & 3 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\
C4 (web) & Pythia       & 2 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\
C4 (web) & OLMo         & 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\
C4 (web) & Qwen-cont    & 3 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\
C4 (web) & Qwen-7B      & 2 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\
C4 (web) & Mistral-7B   & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\
\midrule
arXiv    & Qwen-1.5B-base (cont.) & 4 & 3 & 3 & 2 \\
arXiv    & Pythia-1.4B (cont.)    & 4 & 3 & 3 & 2 \\
\midrule
Cross-scorer & Qwen-cont/Pythia-scored & 2 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\
Cross-scorer & Qwen-cont/OLMo-scored   & 2 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\emph{Structural confound: token length.}
In continuation mode the generation prompt is discarded, so all depths $\geq 1$ consist entirely of machine-generated tokens and the 1v2 boundary compares texts differing only in conditioning context (human-authored vs.\ machine-generated prompt), not structural composition.
Depth~0 texts average ${\sim}$191 subword tokens (C4 truncation), while depth~1+ texts average 193--256 tokens depending on model (Table~\ref{tab:token_length}).
This asymmetry is directionally opposite to a length-inflation confound: shorter depth-0 texts provide less feature-extraction context, making 0v1 discrimination harder, not easier.
Within depths 1--5, mean token counts are stable (within-model range $\leq$ 4 tokens for all configurations except Qwen-continuation at 6 tokens), so the pairwise comparisons underlying $\Kempirical{=}2$ are unaffected by length.
EOS truncation rates are similarly depth-stable within each model: Qwen-rewrite 7--11\%, Pythia 34--36\%, OLMo $<$1\%, Qwen-continuation 17--20\%, Qwen-7B 45--47\%.
Models with the highest truncation (Pythia, Qwen-7B) still yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ with the same step-function AUC decay pattern as low-truncation models (Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci}), confirming that EOS truncation does not drive the boundary.

\emph{Graduated filtering.}
All $\alpha$ values (0.1--0.9) underperform binary-ours on MMLU ($\alpha{=}0.1$: 0.5873; $\alpha{=}0.9$: 0.5892 vs.\ 0.5941); at $\alpha{=}0.9$, HellaSwag matches exactly (0.6701).
Binary-ours significantly outperforms all alternatives on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} \leq 0.021$); Graduated$_{0.9}$ is indistinguishable on HellaSwag ($p = 1.00$) but worse on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} = 0.011$).
\label{tab:pairwise_significance}

\subsection{Dose-Response: Contamination Ratio Effect}
\label{sec:dose_response}

The main filtering experiments (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}) use 100\% synthetic data to maximize effect size.
To characterize behavior at lower contamination ratios, we mix synthetic depths 1--5 with clean C4 at 10\%, 30\%, and 50\% contamination (Qwen-1.5B continuation, 5 seeds each; Table~\ref{tab:dose_response}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{MMLU and HellaSwag by contamination ratio and filtering strategy (5 seeds, mean $\pm$ std). Base model MMLU: 0.6018. Binary filtering retains a significant advantage on HellaSwag across all ratios ($p \leq 0.004$, Holm-Bonferroni corrected).}
\label{tab:dose_response}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{10\%}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{30\%}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{50\%}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\cmidrule(lr){6-7}
\textbf{Strategy} & MMLU & HellaSwag & MMLU & HellaSwag & MMLU & HellaSwag \\
\midrule
No-filter   & $.6058{\pm}.0002$ & $.6689{\pm}.0006$ & $.6069{\pm}.0007$ & $.6664{\pm}.0018$ & $.6063{\pm}.0012$ & $.6680{\pm}.0005$ \\
Binary-ours & $\mathbf{.6079{\pm}.0004}$ & $\mathbf{.6727{\pm}.0007}$ & $\mathbf{.6082{\pm}.0011}$ & $\mathbf{.6725{\pm}.0006}$ & $\mathbf{.6088{\pm}.0009}$ & $\mathbf{.6731{\pm}.0004}$ \\
Graduated   & $.6066{\pm}.0010$ & $.6700{\pm}.0014$ & $.6072{\pm}.0008$ & $.6665{\pm}.0012$ & $.6062{\pm}.0008$ & $.6679{\pm}.0001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

At all tested ratios (10--50\%), no-filter MMLU remains above the base model (0.6018), indicating that synthetic data at these proportions is net-beneficial rather than harmful.
Binary filtering consistently improves over no-filter, with HellaSwag differences significant across all ratios ($p \leq 0.004$, Holm-Bonferroni corrected).
MMLU differences are smaller and significant only at 10\% after correction ($p = 0.001$).
Graduated weighting is indistinguishable from no-filter at all ratios (MMLU $p \geq 0.27$; HellaSwag $p \geq 0.06$), confirming that depth assignments beyond $\Kempirical$ inject noise regardless of contamination severity.
Effect sizes diminish 3--6$\times$ relative to the 100\% setting (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}), but the strategy ordering (binary $>$ graduated $\approx$ no-filter) is preserved.

\subsection{Feature Subset Stability}
\label{sec:feature_subset}

To test whether $\Kempirical{=}2$ depends on the specific 15D feature combination, we evaluate random feature subsets of varying size on Pythia-1.4B prompt-chain data (Table~\ref{tab:feature_subset}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{1v2 $\auc$ by feature subset size (Pythia-1.4B). $\Kempirical{=}2$ ($\auc > \theta{=}0.60$) is robust at $\geq$8 features.}
\label{tab:feature_subset}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Subset} & \textbf{Tested} & \textbf{Pass} & \textbf{Min $\auc$} & \textbf{Mean $\auc$} \\
\midrule
15D (full) & 1 & 1 & 0.6262 & 0.6262 \\
10D        & 5 & 5 & 0.6153 & 0.6191 \\
8D         & 5 & 5 & 0.6056 & 0.6159 \\
5D         & 5 & 2 & 0.5856 & 0.5998 \\
3D (top-3) & 1 & 0 & 0.5871 & 0.5871 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

$\Kempirical{=}2$ holds for all subsets of size $\geq$8 (10/10 pass, min $\auc = 0.6056$), becomes fragile at 5D (2/5 pass), and fails at 3D (top-3 surprisal features alone: $\auc = 0.5871 < \theta$).
The boundary does not depend on any specific feature combination but requires sufficient dimensionality ($\geq$8) to capture the multi-faceted distributional shift between generations 1 and 2.

\subsection{self\_BLEU Feature Independence}
\label{sec:selfbleu_ablation}

To verify that $\Kempirical$ does not depend on the self\_BLEU feature (which may reflect stylistic repetition rather than depth-correlated degradation), we compare 15D and 14D (self\_BLEU removed) classifiers across four model configurations (Table~\ref{tab:selfbleu_ablation}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{15D vs.\ 14D (self\_BLEU removed) 1v2 $\auc$ with bootstrap 95\% CIs. Removing self\_BLEU does not change $\Kempirical$ for any configuration. Maximum $|\Delta\auc| = 0.0031$, within bootstrap CI width (${\sim}0.018$).}
\label{tab:selfbleu_ablation}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & $\Kempirical$ & \textbf{15D $\auc$ [CI]} & \textbf{14D $\auc$ [CI]} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
Qwen-base   & 2/2 & $.6288$ $[.620,\,.638]$ & $.6258$ $[.616,\,.635]$ & $-.0031$ \\
Pythia-1.4B  & 2/2 & $.8840$ $[.878,\,.890]$ & $.8830$ $[.876,\,.889]$ & $-.0010$ \\
LLaMA-8B     & 2/2 & $.6075$ $[.598,\,.617]$ & $.6074$ $[.598,\,.617]$ & $-.0001$ \\
Mistral-7B   & 1/1 & $.5902$ $[.580,\,.600]$ & $.5903$ $[.580,\,.600]$ & $+.0002$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

All four configurations preserve their $\Kempirical$ under 14D, with negligible AUC change ($|\Delta| \leq 0.003$).
The self\_BLEU feature contributes minimally to the depth boundary; $\Kempirical{=}2$ is driven by surprisal and entropy features rather than lexical repetition statistics.

\subsection{Wikipedia Domain: Scorer Confound Resolution}
\label{sec:wiki_domain}

Under same-family scoring (Qwen-1.5B scorer), Wikipedia $\Kempirical$ appeared to exhibit model-family dependence: Qwen generators (1.5B and 7B) yielded $\Kempirical{=}2$, while all four non-Qwen models yielded $\Kempirical{=}1$ (Table~\ref{tab:wiki_domain}).
A scorer confound ablation resolves this apparent split.
Rescoring the same Qwen-7B Wikipedia text with Pythia-1.4B reduces 1v2 $\auc$ from $0.611$ to $0.587$ ($\Delta = -2.4$\,pp), dropping $\Kempirical$ from 2 to 1.
With the scorer confound controlled, all six models yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ on Wikipedia, and the domain ordering simplifies to: arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $>$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Wikipedia pairwise $\auc$ across 6 models. $^\dagger$Qwen rows use same-family scorer (Qwen-1.5B); cross-family scoring (Pythia-1.4B) reduces Qwen-7B to $\Kempirical{=}1$ ($\Delta = -2.4$\,pp), resolving the apparent family split.}
\label{tab:wiki_domain}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Family} & \textbf{0v1} & \textbf{1v2} & \textbf{2v3} & $\Kempirical$ & \textbf{Scorer} \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B (base) & Qwen & ---  & .619 & .550 & 2$^\dagger$ & Qwen-1.5B \\
Qwen-1.5B (base) & Qwen & ---  & .599 & ---  & 1 & Pythia-1.4B \\
Qwen-7B          & Qwen & .967 & .611 & .556 & 2$^\dagger$ & Qwen-1.5B \\
Qwen-7B          & Qwen & ---  & .587 & ---  & 1 & Pythia-1.4B \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B   & non-Qwen & .975 & .585 & .545 & 1 & Qwen-1.5B \\
OLMo-1B       & non-Qwen & .993 & .499 & .501 & 1 & Qwen-1.5B \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B  & non-Qwen & .932 & .595 & .539 & 1 & Qwen-1.5B \\
Mistral-7B    & non-Qwen & .961 & .555 & .524 & 1 & Qwen-1.5B \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$Same-family scorer; cross-family scoring yields $\Kempirical{=}1$.}
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-fold transparency.}
For Qwen-1.5B, cross-family scoring (Pythia-1.4B) yields $\auc$(1v2) $= 0.5994$, with per-fold values $[0.596, 0.606, 0.600, 0.603, 0.592]$ (2/5 folds marginally above $\theta = 0.60$).
The aggregate mean falls below $\theta$, confirming $\Kempirical{=}1$.
For Qwen-7B, the corresponding $\auc$ drops further to $0.587$.

\subsection{ArXiv Cross-Scorer Bootstrap CIs}
\label{sec:arxiv_crossscorer_ci}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Bootstrap 95\% CI for arXiv cross-scorer (Pythia-1.4B) 2v3 boundary $\auc$ (10{,}000 resamples). Point estimates classify 3/4 generators as $\Kempirical{=}3$; strict CI lower-bound criterion confirms 2/4.}
\label{tab:arxiv_crossscorer_ci}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Generator} & \textbf{2v3 $\auc$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{CI lower $> \theta$} \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B    & 0.632 & $[0.617,\,0.646]$ & Yes \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & 0.620 & $[0.605,\,0.636]$ & Yes (0.5\,pp margin) \\
Mistral-7B   & 0.608 & $[0.593,\,0.622]$ & No \\
Qwen-7B      & 0.599 & $[0.584,\,0.613]$ & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{JSD Decay Analysis}
\label{sec:jsd_extended}

The token-level JSD between adjacent generations reveals two distinct decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}).
For C4 web text, 0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$ across all 7 models with $\Kempirical{=}2$ (median cliff ratio ${\sim}$15$\times$), and subsequent pairs converge to a noise floor of ${\sim}$0.003--0.005.
Fitting $\jsd(k, k{+}1) \propto e^{-\lambda k}$ yields $\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$ for C4 (mean ${\approx}\,2.9$), consistent with rapid mixing of $G_\delta$ toward its stationary distribution.
These decay rates represent qualitative characterizations from 5-point curves rather than precise estimates.
The empirical decay rate $\lambda$ is consistent within each domain but varies across domains: C4 yields $\lambda \approx 2.9$, Wikipedia $\lambda \approx 3.1$ (excluding OLMo, whose $\lambda = 6.3$ reflects a capacity-limited collapse: 0v1 $\jsd = 0.169$ with near-complete information loss at the first step, making the exponential fit unreliable), and arXiv $\lambda \approx 1.0$.
This cross-model consistency indicates that the contraction rate of $G_\delta$ is governed primarily by the structure of the input domain rather than the model architecture.
ArXiv text decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower than C4 ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$), with a correspondingly higher convergence floor (${\sim}$0.007--0.009 vs.\ ${\sim}$0.003--0.004).
Formulaic structural features (LaTeX formatting, citation patterns, section templates) degrade more slowly under autoregressive generation and supply persistent depth-distinguishing signal, explaining the elevated $\Kempirical{=}3$.
The retrain-chain exhibits a fundamentally different profile: $\lambda = 0.285$ with a cliff ratio of only 1.31$\times$ and no sharp drop between adjacent pairs.
This gradual, sustained divergence reflects cumulative model-adaptation artifacts rather than text-intrinsic information loss, further supporting the distinction between prompt-chain and retrain-chain $\Kempirical$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:boundary_extended}).

\subsection{Boundary Cases and Scale Effect}
\label{sec:boundary_extended}

Of 8 tested model configurations on C4, 7 yield $\Kempirical{=}2$; Mistral-7B is the sole sub-2 exception ($\Kempirical{=}1$; 1v2 $\auc = 0.594$, CI $[0.584, 0.603]$), validating $\theta{=}0.60$ as an effective discriminator (gap: $0.594$ vs.\ $0.611$--$0.696$).
Mistral's cross-domain profile reinforces the domain-governed interpretation: despite $\Kempirical{=}1$ on both C4 and Wikipedia, it yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv (1v2 $\auc = 0.820$, 2v3 $= 0.628$), and this profile persists under cross-scorer validation (Pythia-1.4B scorer: C4 $\Kempirical{=}1$, arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ (2v3 $\auc = 0.608$, CI $[0.593, 0.622]$)), indicating that domain structural complexity can rescue even web-text one-wash generators (Table~\ref{tab:domain_kstar}).
Larger models produce more human-like text, reducing 0v1 JSD: 0.16--0.29 at $\leq$1.5B, 0.077--0.13 at 7--8B (LLaMA-3.1-8B, Qwen-7B, Mistral-7B).
This trend suggests that models at 13B+ may increasingly yield $\Kempirical{=}1$; this speculative prediction warrants future verification.

